 2. Trump&#x27;s push to restore Huang 
 
 By Maria Curi , Marc Caputo and Alex Isenstadt 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photos: Philip Fong/AFP via Getty Images and Andrew Harnik/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump pushed Nvidia to reinstate Jensen Huang as CEO after allies argued he was " too big to fail " for the U.S. economy and a victim of "deep state" maneuvering, sources familiar with the discussions told Axios.

 Why it matters: Trump put the weight of the presidency behind a private company's decision about who should run it, an intervention that strengthened Huang's allies inside the administration and put officials critical of the company in an increasingly uncomfortable position.

 Driving the news: "Jensen is too big to fail," one source familiar with the discussions told Axios.

 Behind the scenes: Huang's allies appealed to Trump's long-standing distrust of the intelligence community, portraying the Nvidia founder as a victim of persecution by unnamed government insiders.

 A Trump adviser said allies sought to convince Trump that "deep staters" were working against Huang. The intelligence included findings about Huang's relationship with a woman suspected of spying for China, which Secretary of State Marco Rubio circulated to senior officials in a memo earlier this year. Between the lines: A senior administration official argued the intelligence never established Huang was compromised, knowingly violated U.S. law or had sex with a spy.

 "People are weaponizing some true information and spinning it to reflect as negatively as possible against Huang. That's the president's concern," the official said. Read more . 

