 3. Microsoft wants &quot;permission&quot; for next Windows 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Microsoft isn't ready to say when the next major version of Windows will arrive. First, the company wants to make sure people are happy with the one they already have.

 Why it matters: Microsoft has spent years pitching AI as the next great leap in personal computing, but Windows chief Pavan Davuluri tells Axios the company's immediate priority is improving Windows 11's reliability and performance.

 Driving the news: Asked in an interview at Microsoft's Ignite conference last week when Windows 12 might arrive, Davuluri emphasized the work still underway on Windows 11, which he said now has more than a billion users.

 The company's focus is on ensuring "the quality, the craft, the fundamentals, the performance, the reliability" of the operating system while proving the value of its new AI capabilities, he said. Microsoft also needs to build the underlying technology for future AI applications and demonstrate that people actually find those capabilities useful. Only then, Davuluri suggested, does Microsoft "earn permission" to move on to building what's next. Between the lines: Microsoft has faced criticism over Windows 11's reliability and its aggressive efforts to bring AI into the operating system.

 Davuluri didn't rule out Windows 12 or offer a timetable, but his comments suggest Microsoft is focused on strengthening its current operating system rather than rushing out a replacement. Bob O'Donnell, president and chief analyst at Technalysis Research, said Microsoft has recognized the need to address stability and security problems before making another big leap. "There's this general sense of, 'Look, this stuff should be working and it should be working solidly, securely all the time,' and we're still not quite there yet," O'Donnell told Axios.

